 ### Volumetric Backprojection (Monte-Carlo)
-A method for computing high-albedo scattering illumination using Monte-Carlo simulations to solve the integral equation of light transport [S3]. It casts a large set of rays from energy sources into the volume, where at each voxel a "dice is rolled" to determine energy absorption and scattering direction [S3]. After many iterations, the simulation is stopped, and a final scattering of the radiosity volume is performed towards an arbitrarily positioned eye point [S3]. Backprojection is usually performed on a voxel-by-voxel basis [S3].
+A method for computing high-albedo scattering illumination using Monte-Carlo simulations to solve the integral equation of light transport [S4, unofficial]. It casts a large set of rays from energy sources into the volume, where at each voxel a "dice is rolled" to determine energy absorption and scattering direction [S4, unofficial]. After many iterations, the simulation is stopped, and a final scattering of the radiosity volume is performed towards an arbitrarily positioned eye point [S4, unofficial]. Backprojection is usually performed on a voxel-by-voxel basis [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 
 *   **Time Complexity:** High. The method requires many samples to decrease variance because contributing rays are a small fraction of all considered rays [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on implementation. Direct tracing uses minimal memory. Two-pass approaches require storage for the caustic texture map [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on implementation. Direct tracing uses minimal memory. Two-pass approaches require storage for the caustic texture map [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Measured Performance:** Images tend to appear noisy and take a very long time to compute [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Unbiased solution to the radiative transfer equation; handles complex geometries and anisotropic scattering naturally.
-*   **Limitations:** High variance (noise); slow convergence; inefficient for eye-to-light connectivity in highly scattering media.
-*   **When to use:** When unbiased results are required and computational time is not a primary constraint, or when used in hybrid approaches (e.g., two-pass) to capture specific interreflection effects.
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is required or when the volume is highly scattering and direct eye tracing is too noisy.
